  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1219 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nZonal to weak split flow aloft takes hold Wednesday, keeping \nweather quiet, mostly sunny, and pleasant across eastern Oklahoma\nand northwest Arkansas. Afternoon highs Wednesday will reach the \nmid-to-upper 60s to near 70 degrees, right around seasonal \naverages. \n\nA weak shortwave trough will clip the region on Thursday and will\nhelp push another surface cold front through the forecast area. \nHigh-res and global deterministic models depict somewhat limited \nmoisture return ahead of this wave, with the National Blend of \nModels (NBM) showing medium-high PoPs(40-80%)/low-QPF primarily \nacross northwest Arkansas and northeast Oklahoma Thursday \nmorning/afternoon. Any precipitation will remain light and \nscattered (generally a tenth to quarter of an inch), followed \nquickly by another reinforcing shot of dry, seasonably cool air \nThursday night into Friday. \n\nFor the weekend through next Tuesday, consensus in model guidance\nfavor broad mid-level ridging building across the central CONUS. \nSurface high pressure will slowly slide eastward, allowing light \nsoutherly return flow to redevelop by Sunday into early next week.\nThis will support a gradual moderating trend in temperatures, \nwith afternoon highs warming into the low to mid-70s and overnight\nlows comfortably in the 40s to lower 50s by Tuesday/Tuesday \nnight. Overall, high- impact weather remains unlikely through the \nentirety of the extended period.\n\nMejia",
